<!-- ===================== ABSTRACT ===================== -->
<h2>2. Abstract</h2>
<p>
<b>GridGuard</b> is an academic-grade, console-based simulation system developed in modern C++ (C++14 compliant) to model municipal power distribution networks, simulate power line failures, dynamically analyze grid connectivity, prioritize emergency repair operations using max-heaps, calculate optimal repair crew transit routes via Dijkstra's algorithm, and maintain a reversible operational state via stack-based undo mechanisms.
</p>
<p>
The project was built from scratch to demonstrate fundamental Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA) without relying on built-in Standard Template Library (STL) containers for core algorithmic operations. Every foundational data structure — including an Adjacency List Graph, FIFO Queue, LIFO Stack, Binary Max-Heap, Separate-Chaining Hash Table, Doubly Linked List, Custom Dynamic Array, Circular Queue, and Array Stack — is manually engineered using raw pointers, dynamic memory allocation, and explicit rule-of-three memory management.
</p>
<p>
The system models Karachi's power grid with 44 verified geographical locations and 61 simulated power lines, including supply hubs, substations, hospitals, universities, residential neighborhoods, and commercial/industrial zones. An interactive console menu provides 24 operational options, and an automated test suite validates all 10 core scenarios covering normal operation, outages, repair scheduling, undo, Dijkstra routing, hash table collisions, and bidirectional incident history traversal.
</p>
<p>
A React + Leaflet web dashboard frontend provides interactive visualization of the Karachi map, topological graph, Max-Heap triage scheduler, and incident ledger.
</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ===================== INTRODUCTION ===================== -->
<h2>3. Introduction</h2>

<h3>3.1 Background</h3>
<p>
Modern municipal electricity grids rely on interconnected distribution networks featuring generation sources, substations, critical facilities (hospitals, trauma centers), civic infrastructure (universities, transit networks), commercial districts, and residential zones. When severe weather, mechanical breakdown, or physical damage severs transmission lines, grid operators must instantly detect isolated sectors, quantify civic harm, prioritize emergency repair work orders, dispatch repair crews along optimal routes, and systematically re-energize the network while maintaining a reversible operational ledger.
</p>
<p>
Karachi, a megacity of over 15 million residents, presents unique challenges due to its scale, diverse municipal zones, and critical infrastructure distributed across the city. GridGuard addresses these challenges by simulating the Karachi power grid with real geographical coordinates and providing algorithmic tools for fault detection, triage prioritization, and optimal crew dispatch.
</p>

<h3>3.2 Purpose of the Project</h3>
<p>
The primary purpose of GridGuard is to demonstrate mastery of fundamental Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA) by implementing them from scratch without STL container dependencies. Specific objectives include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Manually implementing core data structures (Graph, Queue, Stack, Max-Heap, Hash Table, Doubly Linked List, Custom Vector, Circular Queue, Array Stack) using raw pointers and dynamic memory.</li>
  <li>Implementing graph traversal algorithms (BFS, DFS) for connectivity analysis.</li>
  <li>Implementing Dijkstra's shortest path algorithm for optimal repair crew routing.</li>
  <li>Implementing max-heap priority scheduling for emergency repair triage.</li>
  <li>Implementing manual sorting (QuickSort, MergeSort, Bubble, Selection, Insertion) and searching (Linear, Binary) algorithms.</li>
  <li>Building an interactive console-based simulation with a reversible undo mechanism.</li>
  <li>Providing an automated verification test suite to validate correctness.</li>
</ul>

<h3>3.3 Data Structures Used</h3>
