<!-- ===================== PROBLEM STATEMENT ===================== -->
<h2>4. Problem Statement</h2>

<h3>4.1 What Problem the Project Solves</h3>
<p>
When power line failures occur in a municipal distribution network, grid operators face several challenges:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Fault Detection:</b> Rapidly identifying which locations are de-energized after a line failure in a complex looped/radial topology.</li>
  <li><b>Impact Quantification:</b> Measuring affected population and offline critical facilities (hospitals, trauma centers).</li>
  <li><b>Repair Prioritization:</b> Objectively triaging multiple simultaneous faults based on severity, criticality, population, and outage duration.</li>
  <li><b>Optimal Dispatch:</b> Finding the fastest ground transit route for repair crews that remains accessible even when power lines fail.</li>
  <li><b>Reversible Operations:</b> Maintaining an auditable ledger that allows undoing state-changing actions (failures and repairs).</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.2 Limitations of Current Solutions</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Many grid simulators rely on built-in STL containers, obscuring the underlying data structure mechanics.</li>
  <li>Few educational implementations combine graph connectivity, heap-based triage, Dijkstra routing, and stack-based undo in a single unified system.</li>
  <li>Interactive consoles with reversible operations and automated academic validation are rare in student DSA projects.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4.3 Objectives of the Project</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Implement all core DSA structures manually (no STL containers for core logic).</li>
  <li>Simulate a realistic municipal power grid (Karachi) with 44 locations and 61 power lines.</li>
  <li>Provide BFS/DFS connectivity analysis to detect de-energized sectors.</li>
  <li>Implement Max-Heap priority scheduling for emergency repair triage.</li>
  <li>Implement Dijkstra's algorithm for optimal repair crew routing on an independent road network.</li>
  <li>Provide stack-based undo for reversible operations.</li>
  <li>Implement manual sorting and searching algorithms with complexity demonstrations.</li>
  <li>Validate correctness via a 10-test automated verification suite.</li>
</ol>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ===================== LITERATURE REVIEW ===================== -->
<h2>5. Literature Review / Existing Systems</h2>
<p>
Existing power grid fault detection systems typically fall into three categories:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Commercial SCADA/EMS Systems:</b> Industrial-grade supervisory control and data acquisition systems used by utilities. These are proprietary, expensive, and not designed for educational DSA demonstration.</li>
  <li><b>Open-Source Grid Simulators:</b> Tools like MATPOWER, Pandapower, and OpenDSS focus on power flow analysis rather than data structure education.</li>
  <li><b>Academic DSA Projects:</b> Student projects often implement individual data structures in isolation without integrating them into a cohesive simulation domain.</li>
</ul>
<p>
GridGuard differs by integrating nine manually implemented data structures and six algorithms into a single simulation with an interactive console interface, automated test suite, and optional web dashboard — all while strictly avoiding STL containers for core DSA logic.</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ===================== DATA STRUCTURES USED ===================== -->
<h2>6. Data Structures Used</h2>

<h3>6.1 Graph (Adjacency List)</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> The power grid is naturally a graph where locations are vertices and transmission lines are edges. An adjacency list provides efficient traversal and edge lookups.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/Graph.h</code>, <code>src/Graph.cpp</code> — Models the Karachi power distribution topology.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>addLocation</code>, <code>addPowerLine</code>, <code>disablePowerLine</code>, <code>restorePowerLine</code>, <code>analyzeConnectivity</code>, <code>runBFS</code>, <code>runDFS</code>, <code>getLocation</code>, <code>getPowerLine</code>.</p>

<h3>6.2 Queue (Singly Linked List FIFO)</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> BFS requires a FIFO structure to explore vertices level by level.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/Queue.h</code> — Powers Breadth-First Search in <code>Graph::runBFS</code>.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>enqueue</code>, <code>dequeue</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>isEmpty</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>display</code>.</p>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> Enqueue O(1), Dequeue O(1), Peek O(1). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n).</p>

<h3>6.3 Circular Queue (Array-Based Ring Buffer)</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> Provides O(1) enqueue/dequeue with array storage and modulo wraparound, reusing empty slots efficiently.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/CircularQueue.h</code> — Alternative BFS queue implementation.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>enqueue</code>, <code>dequeue</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>isEmpty</code>, <code>isFull</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>display</code>.</p>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> All operations O(1). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(capacity).</p>

<h3>6.4 Stack (Singly Linked List LIFO)</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> The undo mechanism requires LIFO ordering to reverse the most recent state-changing operation first.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/Stack.h</code> — Undo operation history.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>isEmpty</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>displayHistory</code>.</p>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> All operations O(1). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n).</p>

<h3>6.5 Array Stack (Dynamically Resizing Array)</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> Array-based stack with dynamic doubling provides better cache locality than linked-list stack.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/ArrayStack.h</code> — Undo log storage in <code>Simulation</code>.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>isEmpty</code>, <code>isFull</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>capacity</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>forEachTopDown</code>.</p>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> Push O(1) amortized, Pop O(1), Peek O(1). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n).</p>

<h3>6.6 Priority Queue (Binary Max-Heap)</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> Emergency repair triage requires extracting the maximum-priority fault in O(log n) time.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/MaxHeap.h</code> — Repair queue in <code>Simulation</code>.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>insert</code>, <code>extractMax</code>, <code>peekMax</code>, <code>heapifyUp</code>, <code>heapifyDown</code>, <code>displayQueue</code>, <code>updateFaultStatus</code>, <code>findFault</code>.</p>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> Insert O(log n), ExtractMax O(log n), PeekMax O(1). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n).</p>

<h3>6.7 Hash Table (Separate Chaining)</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> Provides average O(1) lookup for locations and fault records by ID.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/HashTable.h</code> — Location and fault lookups in <code>Simulation</code>.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>insert</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>searchPtr</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>getCollisionCount</code>, <code>displayBucketDiagnostics</code>.</p>
<p><b>Hash Function:</b> Integer keys: <code>key % capacity</code>. String keys: djb2 polynomial rolling hash. <b>Time Complexity:</b> Average O(1), Worst O(n). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n + capacity).</p>

<h3>6.8 Doubly Linked List</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> Incident history requires bidirectional traversal (chronological and reverse-chronological) and O(1) removal from both ends.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/DoublyLinkedList.h</code> — Incident event logging in <code>Simulation</code>.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>append</code>, <code>prepend</code>, <code>removeFirst</code>, <code>removeLast</code>, <code>displayForward</code>, <code>displayBackward</code>, <code>searchEvents</code>, <code>getLatest</code>.</p>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> Append/Prepend O(1), RemoveFirst/RemoveLast O(1), Search O(n). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n).</p>

<h3>6.9 Custom Vector (Dynamic Array)</h3>
<p><b>Why chosen:</b> Provides dynamic array functionality without STL <code>std::vector</code>, adhering to the zero-STL policy.</p>
<p><b>Where used:</b> <code>include/CustomVector.h</code> — General-purpose dynamic arrays throughout the project.</p>
<p><b>Operations implemented:</b> <code>push_back</code>, <code>pop_back</code>, <code>operator[]</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>capacity</code>, <code>empty</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>begin</code>, <code>end</code>.</p>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> Push_back O(1) amortized, Access O(1). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n).</p>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ===================== ALGORITHMS USED ===================== -->
<h2>7. Algorithms Used</h2>

<h3>7.1 Breadth-First Search (BFS)</h3>
<p><b>Explanation:</b> BFS explores the power grid starting from the Main Power Station using a custom Queue, visiting all reachable locations via operational transmission lines. This partitions the city into powered versus disconnected sectors.</p>
<p><b>Pseudocode:</b></p>
<div class="code-block">
BFS(startVertex):
  visited[startVertex] = true
  Queue.enqueue(startVertex)
  while Queue not empty:
    u = Queue.dequeue()
    reachable.add(u)
    for each neighbor v of u:
      if edge (u,v) is operational AND not visited[v]:
        visited[v] = true
        Queue.enqueue(v)
  return reachable
</div>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> O(V + E) where V = vertices, E = edges. <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(V).</p>

<h3>7.2 Depth-First Search (DFS)</h3>
<p><b>Explanation:</b> DFS recursively traverses the network from a start vertex, exploring as far as possible along each branch before backtracking. Used for topological reachability inspection.</p>
<p><b>Pseudocode:</b></p>
<div class="code-block">
DFS(u, visited):
  visited[u] = true
  traversalOrder.add(u)
  for each neighbor v of u:
    if edge (u,v) is operational AND not visited[v]:
      DFS(v, visited)
</div>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> O(V + E). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(V) for recursion stack + visited array.</p>

<h3>7.3 Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm</h3>
<p><b>Explanation:</b> Dijkstra's algorithm computes the minimum-travel-time path from a repair crew depot to a target location on the independent road network. It uses positive edge weights (travel minutes) and reconstructs the path via parent pointers.</p>
<p><b>Pseudocode:</b></p>
<div class="code-block">
Dijkstra(startId, targetId):
  dist[startId] = 0; all others = INF
  visited[all] = false; parent[all] = -1
  repeat V times:
    u = unvisited vertex with minimum dist[u]
    if u == -1 or dist[u] == INF: break
    visited[u] = true
    if u == targetId: break
    for each neighbor v of u:
      if not visited[v] and dist[u] + weight(u,v) < dist[v]:
        dist[v] = dist[u] + weight(u,v)
        parent[v] = u
  reconstruct path from targetId using parent pointers
</div>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> O(V²) with array-based implementation (no priority queue). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(V).</p>

<h3>7.4 Max-Heap Priority Scheduling</h3>
<p><b>Explanation:</b> Faults are inserted into a binary max-heap ordered by priority score. The highest-priority fault is at the root and extracted first for repair.</p>
<p><b>Priority Score Formula:</b></p>
<div class="code-block">
Priority Score = (Severity × 10) + (Critical Facilities × 20)
               + (Population Affected / 100) + (Outage Duration × 2)
</div>
<p><b>Pseudocode:</b></p>
<div class="code-block>
MaxHeapInsert(fault):
  heap[heapSize] = fault
  heapifyUp(heapSize)
  heapSize++

MaxHeapExtractMax():
  maxItem = heap[0]
  heap[0] = heap[heapSize - 1]
  heapSize--
  heapifyDown(0)
  return maxItem

heapifyUp(i):
  while i > 0 and heap[i] > heap[parent(i)]:
    swap(heap[i], heap[parent(i)])
    i = parent(i)

heapifyDown(i):
  largest = i
  if left child > heap[largest]: largest = left
  if right child > heap[largest]: largest = right
  if largest != i:
    swap(heap[i], heap[largest])
    heapifyDown(largest)
</div>
<p><b>Time Complexity:</b> Insert O(log n), ExtractMax O(log n), PeekMax O(1). <b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n).</p>

<h3>7.5 Sorting Algorithms</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Best Case</th><th>Average Case</th><th>Worst Case</th><th>Space Complexity</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Bubble Sort</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Selection Sort</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Insertion Sort</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(1)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Quick Sort</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Merge Sort</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n log n)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>
</table>

<p><b>QuickSort Pseudocode:</b></p>
<div class="code-block>
quickSort(arr, low, high):
  if low < high:
    pi = partition(arr, low, high)
    quickSort(arr, low, pi - 1)
    quickSort(arr, pi + 1, high)

partition(arr, low, high):
  pivot = arr[high]
  i = low - 1
  for j = low to high - 1:
    if compare(arr[j], pivot):
      i++
      swap(arr[i], arr[j])
  swap(arr[i + 1], arr[high])
  return i + 1
</div>

<p><b>MergeSort Pseudocode:</b></p>
<div class="code-block>
mergeSort(arr, left, right):
  if left < right:
    mid = (left + right) / 2
    mergeSort(arr, left, mid)
    mergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right)
    merge(arr, left, mid, right)
</div>

<h3>7.6 Searching Algorithms</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Time Complexity</th><th>Space Complexity</th><th>Requirement</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Linear Search</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Works on unsorted data</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Binary Search</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Requires sorted data</td></tr>
</table>

