<h3>9.3 Discussion</h3>
<p>
The manual implementation of all data structures without STL containers demonstrates a deep understanding of memory management, pointer manipulation, and algorithmic complexity. Key findings:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Zero-STL Policy:</b> All core DSA logic uses raw pointers and dynamic memory. Only <code>std::string</code>, streams, and exceptions are used for text/I/O support.</li>
  <li><b>Memory Safety:</b> All classes implement the rule-of-three (destructor, copy constructor, copy assignment) to prevent memory leaks and double-free errors.</li>
  <li><b>Algorithmic Correctness:</b> All 10 test scenarios pass, validating BFS connectivity, heap prioritization, Dijkstra routing, hash collisions, and undo operations.</li>
  <li><b>Time Complexity:</b> Operations match theoretical expectations — BFS/DFS O(V+E), Dijkstra O(V²), Heap O(log n), Hash Table O(1) average, Sorting O(n log n) for Quick/Merge, O(n²) for Bubble/Selection/Insertion.</li>
</ul>

<div class="page-break"></div>

<!-- ===================== CONCLUSION ===================== -->
<h2>10. Conclusion</h2>
<p>
GridGuard successfully demonstrates the implementation and integration of nine manually engineered data structures and six fundamental algorithms in a unified power grid fault detection and restoration simulation. The project achieves all stated objectives:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Core DSA structures (Graph, Queue, Stack, Max-Heap, Hash Table, Doubly Linked List, Custom Vector, Circular Queue, Array Stack) are implemented from scratch without STL containers.</li>
  <li>Graph traversal (BFS, DFS) correctly identifies powered versus disconnected sectors.</li>
  <li>Dijkstra's algorithm computes optimal repair crew routes on an independent road network.</li>
  <li>Max-Heap priority scheduling objectively triages emergency repairs using the defined scoring formula.</li>
  <li>Stack-based undo enables reversible state-changing operations with full audit trail.</li>
  <li>Manual sorting (5 algorithms) and searching (2 algorithms) are provided with complexity demonstrations.</li>
  <li>All 10 automated validation tests pass, confirming correctness across normal operation, outages, repairs, undo, routing, hashing, and history traversal.</li>
</ul>
<p>
The project serves as a comprehensive academic demonstration of Data Structures and Algorithms principles applied to a realistic municipal infrastructure domain.</p>

